# Release 0.0.6: general local ZFS provisioning

Release 0.0.6 adds an opt-in, locally executed ZFS workflow. It discovers
whole disks, helps an operator select and group them, writes an editable
`zpool.config`, validates that declaration against current hardware, and
creates only the exact reviewed plan.

The feature has no hostname, IP address, fixed disk count, fixed transport, or
fixed pool-topology dependency. It is not part of the automatic Proxmox
bootstrap playlist.

## Configuration workflow

The streamed entry point defaults to the non-destructive configuration action
when its first argument is a flag:

```bash
mkdir -p /root/zfs-setup
cd /root/zfs-setup
wget -qO- https://devs-guide.github.io/proxmox/setup/storage/zfs.sh \
  | bash -s -- --type sas --size 6TB
```

The inventory table contains kernel and stable paths, model, serial, WWN,
transport, media type, exact byte size, SMART state, signatures, eligibility,
and discoverable controller/enclosure evidence. Eligible candidates are
confirmed with `y`, `n`, `all`, or `none`. The operator then confirms the vdev
type, equal-width vdev count, device order, dataset, and mountpoint.

SMART health is normalized to exactly one value: `passed`, `failed`, or
`unknown`. It is advisory by default and does not override the hard safety
checks for active usage, stable identity, whole-disk status, or signatures.
Operators may explicitly choose `smart-required` or attach BHT inventory as
advisory context. `bht-required` and evidence-package checksum verification are
also available, but both are opt-in and are not part of the normal workflow.

For deterministic automation, combine an ordered `--serial` array/file,
repeatable `--device`, or
`--all-matches` with `--non-interactive`, `--vdev-type`, and either
`--vdev-count` or `--drives-per-vdev`. A filtered result larger than the
requested topology is never silently truncated.

Exact normalized model filters can be repeated with `--model`. `--serial`
accepts either `[SERIAL_01,SERIAL_02,...]` or a text file containing one serial
per line; supplied order becomes vdev member order without relying on
transient `/dev/sdX` names.

Supported data-vdev types are `mirror`, `raidz1`, `raidz2`, and `raidz3`.
Unmirrored stripes, special vdevs, cache, log, and hot-spare creation are out of
scope for this release.

Size units are explicit. `6TB` is decimal while `5.5TiB` is binary. Ambiguous
forms such as `5.5T` are rejected. The generated filter stores minimum and
maximum bytes, while each selected device records its exact expected byte size,
WWN, serial, transport, and sector sizes.

## Manual review and planning

The generator writes `./zpool.config` with mode `0600` and stops. Edit that
JSON before continuing if device ordering, grouping, properties, datasets, or
mountpoints need adjustment.

Download the runner before planning or performing any destructive action:

```bash
wget -qO zfs.sh https://devs-guide.github.io/proxmox/setup/storage/zfs.sh
chmod 0700 zfs.sh
./zfs.sh validate-config --config ./zpool.config
./zfs.sh preflight --config ./zpool.config
./zfs.sh plan --config ./zpool.config --plan-file ./zpool.plan
```

Validation enforces supported equal-width topology, unique stable by-id paths,
whole disks, matching identities and sizes, safe property allow-lists, and
valid datasets. Live preflight additionally rejects mounted filesystems, swap,
LVM, mdraid, Ceph, imported ZFS members, device holders, identity drift, and an
existing or importable pool with the requested name.

Planning prints the topology, conservative capacity estimates, exact commands,
and a physical fault-domain warning. It runs `zpool create -n`, then writes a
canonical `zpool.plan` and SHA-256 plan ID. Editing the configuration or changing
resolved hardware invalidates that plan.

The generated configuration records observed HBAs and emits a `single-hba`
advisory when applicable. That advisory requires human review of expanders,
backplanes, enclosure slots, and power domains but does not guess or impose a
physical layout.

## Explicit destructive actions

Detected signatures stop ordinary planning. Setting
`allow_signature_wipe: true` permits a separate signature-wipe plan; it does
not permit creation to erase anything automatically.

```bash
./zfs.sh wipe-signatures \
  --plan-file ./zpool.plan --plan-id PLAN_ID \
  --mode wipe-signatures --confirm-wipe POOL_NAME
```

Without `--yes`, the wipe workflow confirms every affected stable path. After
wiping, inventory and planning must run again.

Creation requires the fresh create-plan ID, explicit mode, exact pool name, and
an interactive final confirmation. Non-interactive creation additionally
requires `--yes`:

```bash
./zfs.sh apply \
  --plan-file ./zpool.plan --plan-id PLAN_ID \
  --mode create --confirm-create POOL_NAME
```

The tool never adds `zpool create -f`. After creation it verifies data-vdev
type, width, device membership, pool properties, datasets, and mounted state.

## Release boundary

This release contains only the general ZFS tooling. Samba and LXC provisioning,
network attachment, SSH access, Node.js, and server-specific storage choices
remain separate tasks built from existing features. GPU work remains targeted
for release 0.0.7.

The step-by-step review gates are documented in the
[human acceptance procedure](human.acceptance.md).
